  {
    "topicTitle": "Declarative Pipeline",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "s03-dp-01",
        "title": "Declarative Pipeline Overview and Design Philosophy",
        "content": "Declarative Pipeline provides a structured way to define Jenkins automation using a recognizable set of required and optional sections. Its syntax encourages consistent organization of agents, stages, environment values, parameters, options, and post-build actions. This structure makes pipelines easier to review and standardize across teams. Declarative Pipeline is particularly useful when the workflow follows a clear sequence of build, test, package, and deployment activities.\n\nImplementation, verification, and operational practice:\nCreate a minimal Declarative Pipeline and identify its top-level pipeline, agent, stages, and steps blocks. Validate the Jenkinsfile and run it on a test agent. Explain how the structure makes the workflow visible to reviewers.\n\nIn a production Jenkins environment, validate this behavior with a controlled test pipeline before enabling it for critical workloads. Record the configuration, repository or job involved, expected result, actual result, and relevant console output. Review permissions, credential exposure, network dependencies, agent requirements, and failure behavior. Prefer version-controlled configuration and repeatable procedures over undocumented manual changes. When a failure occurs, preserve the original evidence, identify the first failing operation, correct the underlying cause, and rerun the smallest safe validation before restarting the complete delivery workflow."
      },
      {
        "id": "s03-dp-02",
        "title": "Required Pipeline Block and Declarative Structure",
        "content": "A Declarative Pipeline begins with a pipeline block that contains the workflow definition. Required structures include an agent declaration and a stages section, while additional sections can configure environment variables, parameters, options, triggers, tools, and post actions. Declarative syntax has validation rules that prevent many structurally invalid definitions from executing. Understanding these rules helps developers identify errors before a build begins.\n\nImplementation, verification, and operational practice:\nReview a valid pipeline and compare it with examples that omit required structures or place blocks incorrectly. Run the Jenkinsfile validator and document the syntax error messages. Keep configuration blocks at the correct level.\n\nIn a production Jenkins environment, validate this behavior with a controlled test pipeline before enabling it for critical workloads. Record the configuration, repository or job involved, expected result, actual result, and relevant console output. Review permissions, credential exposure, network dependencies, agent requirements, and failure behavior. Prefer version-controlled configuration and repeatable procedures over undocumented manual changes. When a failure occurs, preserve the original evidence, identify the first failing operation, correct the underlying cause, and rerun the smallest safe validation before restarting the complete delivery workflow."
      },
      {
        "id": "s03-dp-03",
        "title": "Pipeline-Level Agent Configuration",
        "content": "A pipeline-level agent selects the execution environment for the workflow. The agent may be any available executor, a node matching a label, or another supported execution environment. Selecting an appropriate agent ensures that all stages have access to the required operating system, build tools, workspace, and network resources. The agent choice also affects resource consumption and the security boundary of the pipeline.\n\nImplementation, verification, and operational practice:\nConfigure a pipeline-level agent using a test label. Print the node name and inspect the workspace. Verify that the selected agent has the required build tools and does not rely on software installed only on the controller.\n\nIn a production Jenkins environment, validate this behavior with a controlled test pipeline before enabling it for critical workloads. Record the configuration, repository or job involved, expected result, actual result, and relevant console output. Review permissions, credential exposure, network dependencies, agent requirements, and failure behavior. Prefer version-controlled configuration and repeatable procedures over undocumented manual changes. When a failure occurs, preserve the original evidence, identify the first failing operation, correct the underlying cause, and rerun the smallest safe validation before restarting the complete delivery workflow."
      },
      {
        "id": "s03-dp-04",
        "title": "Agent None and Stage-Level Agents",
        "content": "The agent none configuration allows a pipeline to avoid reserving one executor for the entire workflow. Individual stages can then declare their own agents. This is useful when different stages require different operating systems or specialized tools, or when a pipeline should allocate capacity only while a stage is running. Stage-level agents can introduce workspace and artifact-transfer considerations that must be handled deliberately.\n\nImplementation, verification, and operational practice:\nBuild a pipeline with agent none and separate Linux and deployment stages using suitable labels. Verify where each stage runs and how files are transferred or stashed between workspaces. Observe executor usage while stages execute.\n\nIn a production Jenkins environment, validate this behavior with a controlled test pipeline before enabling it for critical workloads. Record the configuration, repository or job involved, expected result, actual result, and relevant console output. Review permissions, credential exposure, network dependencies, agent requirements, and failure behavior. Prefer version-controlled configuration and repeatable procedures over undocumented manual changes. When a failure occurs, preserve the original evidence, identify the first failing operation, correct the underlying cause, and rerun the smallest safe validation before restarting the complete delivery workflow."
      },
      {
        "id": "s03-dp-05",
        "title": "Stages and Nested Stage Organization",
        "content": "The stages section contains the major phases of a Declarative Pipeline. Each stage has a name and a steps block or supported nested stage structure. Stage names should communicate business or engineering purpose, such as Build Application, Run Unit Tests, or Publish Image. Clear stage boundaries improve visualization and make it easier to locate the operation responsible for a failure.\n\nImplementation, verification, and operational practice:\nCreate a pipeline with checkout, build, test, and package stages. Use descriptive names and verify the stage view. Add a controlled failure in one stage and identify how the pipeline reports later stages.\n\nIn a production Jenkins environment, validate this behavior with a controlled test pipeline before enabling it for critical workloads. Record the configuration, repository or job involved, expected result, actual result, and relevant console output. Review permissions, credential exposure, network dependencies, agent requirements, and failure behavior. Prefer version-controlled configuration and repeatable procedures over undocumented manual changes. When a failure occurs, preserve the original evidence, identify the first failing operation, correct the underlying cause, and rerun the smallest safe validation before restarting the complete delivery workflow."
      },
      {
        "id": "s03-dp-06",
        "title": "Steps Block and Common Pipeline Steps",
        "content": "The steps block contains the operations performed by a stage. Common pipeline steps include shell execution, batch execution, source checkout, file handling, and plugin-provided integration actions. The steps block should contain commands that belong to the stage's responsibility. Long shell scripts can be moved into version-controlled build scripts to keep Jenkinsfile logic readable and testable.\n\nImplementation, verification, and operational practice:\nCreate stages using echo, sh or bat, checkout, and a harmless file operation as appropriate for the agent operating system. Confirm that every command executes in the expected workspace and that a non-zero command exit status is reflected in the build result.\n\nIn a production Jenkins environment, validate this behavior with a controlled test pipeline before enabling it for critical workloads. Record the configuration, repository or job involved, expected result, actual result, and relevant console output. Review permissions, credential exposure, network dependencies, agent requirements, and failure behavior. Prefer version-controlled configuration and repeatable procedures over undocumented manual changes. When a failure occurs, preserve the original evidence, identify the first failing operation, correct the underlying cause, and rerun the smallest safe validation before restarting the complete delivery workflow."
      },
      {
        "id": "s03-dp-07",
        "title": "Environment Directive and Variable Scope",
        "content": "The environment directive defines environment variables for a pipeline or a specific stage. Pipeline-level variables are available across the relevant workflow, while stage-level variables limit configuration to a smaller execution area. Environment variables are useful for application names, build modes, and non-sensitive environment configuration. Secret values should be supplied through credential bindings rather than written directly into the Jenkinsfile.\n\nImplementation, verification, and operational practice:\nDefine a non-sensitive application variable at pipeline level and a separate variable at stage level. Verify their availability in the intended stages. Attempt to use a stage-only value elsewhere and document the scope behavior.\n\nIn a production Jenkins environment, validate this behavior with a controlled test pipeline before enabling it for critical workloads. Record the configuration, repository or job involved, expected result, actual result, and relevant console output. Review permissions, credential exposure, network dependencies, agent requirements, and failure behavior. Prefer version-controlled configuration and repeatable procedures over undocumented manual changes. When a failure occurs, preserve the original evidence, identify the first failing operation, correct the underlying cause, and rerun the smallest safe validation before restarting the complete delivery workflow."
      },
      {
        "id": "s03-dp-08",
        "title": "Parameters Directive and Input Validation",
        "content": "The parameters directive defines values that users can supply when starting a pipeline. Supported parameter types can include strings, booleans, choices, and other types provided by Jenkins or plugins. Parameters make a pipeline flexible but should be validated before they influence commands or deployment targets. A free-form parameter must not be trusted as a safe shell fragment or authorization decision.\n\nImplementation, verification, and operational practice:\nCreate choice and boolean parameters for a test workflow. Add validation that rejects an unsupported deployment target. Confirm how Jenkins presents parameter values and ensure that sensitive credentials are not represented as ordinary parameters.\n\nIn a production Jenkins environment, validate this behavior with a controlled test pipeline before enabling it for critical workloads. Record the configuration, repository or job involved, expected result, actual result, and relevant console output. Review permissions, credential exposure, network dependencies, agent requirements, and failure behavior. Prefer version-controlled configuration and repeatable procedures over undocumented manual changes. When a failure occurs, preserve the original evidence, identify the first failing operation, correct the underlying cause, and rerun the smallest safe validation before restarting the complete delivery workflow."
      },
      {
        "id": "s03-dp-09",
        "title": "Options Directive and Pipeline Execution Controls",
        "content": "The options directive configures behavior such as build timeouts, concurrency controls, timestamps, and retention policies where supported. These options help protect Jenkins from indefinitely running builds and uncontrolled resource use. Options should be selected according to workload behavior; for example, a long integration test may need a different timeout from a short lint stage. Retention settings should also align with audit and troubleshooting requirements.\n\nImplementation, verification, and operational practice:\nConfigure a reasonable timeout, timestamped logs, and a build retention policy in a test pipeline. Verify the timeout behavior with a controlled long-running step and confirm that build history follows the intended retention rule.\n\nIn a production Jenkins environment, validate this behavior with a controlled test pipeline before enabling it for critical workloads. Record the configuration, repository or job involved, expected result, actual result, and relevant console output. Review permissions, credential exposure, network dependencies, agent requirements, and failure behavior. Prefer version-controlled configuration and repeatable procedures over undocumented manual changes. When a failure occurs, preserve the original evidence, identify the first failing operation, correct the underlying cause, and rerun the smallest safe validation before restarting the complete delivery workflow."
      },
      {
        "id": "s03-dp-10",
        "title": "Triggers Directive and Scheduled Execution",
        "content": "The triggers directive defines automated events that can start a pipeline, such as scheduled execution or supported polling mechanisms. Repository webhooks are often configured through source-control integrations rather than relying on a timer. Trigger selection should avoid unnecessary builds and should reflect the purpose of the workflow. A nightly maintenance pipeline may need a schedule, while a source validation pipeline may be event-driven.\n\nImplementation, verification, and operational practice:\nConfigure a harmless scheduled pipeline and inspect its next execution behavior. Compare timer-based execution with webhook-driven execution. Verify that scheduled builds use the intended branch and do not deploy to production without the required authorization.\n\nIn a production Jenkins environment, validate this behavior with a controlled test pipeline before enabling it for critical workloads. Record the configuration, repository or job involved, expected result, actual result, and relevant console output. Review permissions, credential exposure, network dependencies, agent requirements, and failure behavior. Prefer version-controlled configuration and repeatable procedures over undocumented manual changes. When a failure occurs, preserve the original evidence, identify the first failing operation, correct the underlying cause, and rerun the smallest safe validation before restarting the complete delivery workflow."
      },
      {
        "id": "s03-dp-11",
        "title": "Tools Directive and Build Tool Installation",
        "content": "The tools directive can select configured installations for supported build tools, depending on Jenkins configuration and available plugins. This allows a pipeline to request a known tool installation rather than assuming that an arbitrary version exists in the agent PATH. Tool configuration should be consistent across environments and should be tested after Jenkins or agent upgrades.\n\nImplementation, verification, and operational practice:\nConfigure a test JDK or Maven installation in Jenkins and reference it from a Declarative Pipeline. Print the selected tool version and verify that the correct executable is used on the assigned agent.\n\nIn a production Jenkins environment, validate this behavior with a controlled test pipeline before enabling it for critical workloads. Record the configuration, repository or job involved, expected result, actual result, and relevant console output. Review permissions, credential exposure, network dependencies, agent requirements, and failure behavior. Prefer version-controlled configuration and repeatable procedures over undocumented manual changes. When a failure occurs, preserve the original evidence, identify the first failing operation, correct the underlying cause, and rerun the smallest safe validation before restarting the complete delivery workflow."
      },
      {
        "id": "s03-dp-12",
        "title": "When Directive and Conditional Stage Execution",
        "content": "The when directive controls whether a stage should run based on conditions such as branch, tag, change request, environment, or expression. Conditions allow one Jenkinsfile to support multiple branches and release workflows without duplicating entire jobs. Conditions must be reviewed carefully because an incorrect expression can skip security checks or deploy from an unintended branch.\n\nImplementation, verification, and operational practice:\nCreate separate test, packaging, and release stages with branch-based conditions. Execute the pipeline on matching and non-matching branches. Verify skipped-stage behavior and ensure required validation stages cannot be bypassed accidentally.\n\nIn a production Jenkins environment, validate this behavior with a controlled test pipeline before enabling it for critical workloads. Record the configuration, repository or job involved, expected result, actual result, and relevant console output. Review permissions, credential exposure, network dependencies, agent requirements, and failure behavior. Prefer version-controlled configuration and repeatable procedures over undocumented manual changes. When a failure occurs, preserve the original evidence, identify the first failing operation, correct the underlying cause, and rerun the smallest safe validation before restarting the complete delivery workflow."
      },
      {
        "id": "s03-dp-13",
        "title": "Post Directive and Build Result Conditions",
        "content": "The post directive defines actions that execute after the pipeline or a stage completes. Conditions such as always, success, failure, unstable, and changed support cleanup, reporting, and notification behavior. Post actions should be designed so they do not conceal the original result. A notification failure should be handled separately from a failed application test or deployment.\n\nImplementation, verification, and operational practice:\nConfigure post actions for success, failure, and always. Use harmless test messages and a cleanup operation. Trigger both a successful and unsuccessful build and verify which post conditions execute.\n\nIn a production Jenkins environment, validate this behavior with a controlled test pipeline before enabling it for critical workloads. Record the configuration, repository or job involved, expected result, actual result, and relevant console output. Review permissions, credential exposure, network dependencies, agent requirements, and failure behavior. Prefer version-controlled configuration and repeatable procedures over undocumented manual changes. When a failure occurs, preserve the original evidence, identify the first failing operation, correct the underlying cause, and rerun the smallest safe validation before restarting the complete delivery workflow."
      },
      {
        "id": "s03-dp-14",
        "title": "Parallel Declarative Stages",
        "content": "Declarative Pipeline supports parallel execution so independent work can run concurrently. Typical examples include unit tests on multiple runtime versions, operating-system compatibility checks, and independent security scans. Parallel execution can reduce total duration but increases executor and resource consumption. Tasks that depend on one another should remain sequential, and parallel branches should not write conflicting files into the same workspace.\n\nImplementation, verification, and operational practice:\nCreate two independent test branches using the supported parallel stage syntax. Compare execution duration with a sequential implementation. Verify that each branch has appropriate resource allocation and that one branch's failure is reported clearly.\n\nIn a production Jenkins environment, validate this behavior with a controlled test pipeline before enabling it for critical workloads. Record the configuration, repository or job involved, expected result, actual result, and relevant console output. Review permissions, credential exposure, network dependencies, agent requirements, and failure behavior. Prefer version-controlled configuration and repeatable procedures over undocumented manual changes. When a failure occurs, preserve the original evidence, identify the first failing operation, correct the underlying cause, and rerun the smallest safe validation before restarting the complete delivery workflow."
      },
      {
        "id": "s03-dp-15",
        "title": "Matrix Builds and Multi-Configuration Testing",
        "content": "Matrix execution runs a stage across combinations of defined axes, such as operating system, runtime version, or application configuration. It helps teams validate compatibility without manually duplicating similar stages. The number of combinations can grow quickly, so matrix design should include only meaningful combinations and should account for executor capacity and test duration.\n\nImplementation, verification, and operational practice:\nDesign a small matrix with two runtime versions and two test configurations. Inspect the generated combinations and verify that each run reports its axis values. Estimate the total executor demand before expanding the matrix for production use.\n\nIn a production Jenkins environment, validate this behavior with a controlled test pipeline before enabling it for critical workloads. Record the configuration, repository or job involved, expected result, actual result, and relevant console output. Review permissions, credential exposure, network dependencies, agent requirements, and failure behavior. Prefer version-controlled configuration and repeatable procedures over undocumented manual changes. When a failure occurs, preserve the original evidence, identify the first failing operation, correct the underlying cause, and rerun the smallest safe validation before restarting the complete delivery workflow."
      },
      {
        "id": "s03-dp-16",
        "title": "Input Directive and Manual Approval Gates",
        "content": "The input directive can pause a pipeline and request a human decision before continuing. Approval gates are commonly used before production deployments or other high-impact operations. Input permissions and approver identity should be configured carefully so that only authorized users can approve the release. A pipeline should record the approval context and should not treat a timeout or rejected input as a successful deployment.\n\nImplementation, verification, and operational practice:\nAdd a test approval step before a harmless deployment simulation. Verify who can approve, how the pipeline resumes, and what happens when the input is rejected or times out. Keep production credentials unavailable until the approved deployment stage actually begins.\n\nIn a production Jenkins environment, validate this behavior with a controlled test pipeline before enabling it for critical workloads. Record the configuration, repository or job involved, expected result, actual result, and relevant console output. Review permissions, credential exposure, network dependencies, agent requirements, and failure behavior. Prefer version-controlled configuration and repeatable procedures over undocumented manual changes. When a failure occurs, preserve the original evidence, identify the first failing operation, correct the underlying cause, and rerun the smallest safe validation before restarting the complete delivery workflow."
      },
      {
        "id": "s03-dp-17",
        "title": "Declarative Error Handling and Retry Controls",
        "content": "Declarative Pipeline can use supported options and steps to manage timeouts, retries, and failure behavior. Retry is appropriate for transient operations such as a temporary network interruption, but it should not hide invalid configuration or failed quality gates. Error handling must preserve meaningful build results and provide enough context for operators to understand why a stage failed.\n\nImplementation, verification, and operational practice:\nTest a retry around a simulated transient operation and a timeout around a delayed step. Compare the resulting logs and build statuses. Keep test failures and security violations as genuine failures rather than converting them into success.\n\nIn a production Jenkins environment, validate this behavior with a controlled test pipeline before enabling it for critical workloads. Record the configuration, repository or job involved, expected result, actual result, and relevant console output. Review permissions, credential exposure, network dependencies, agent requirements, and failure behavior. Prefer version-controlled configuration and repeatable procedures over undocumented manual changes. When a failure occurs, preserve the original evidence, identify the first failing operation, correct the underlying cause, and rerun the smallest safe validation before restarting the complete delivery workflow."
      },
      {
        "id": "s03-dp-18",
        "title": "Declarative Pipeline Validation and Production Standards",
        "content": "Production Declarative Pipelines should follow consistent naming, stage boundaries, credential handling, agent selection, timeout policy, retention, and approval practices. Jenkinsfile validation helps detect syntax errors, while pipeline execution tests verify actual plugin and environment behavior. Teams should review pipeline changes through version control and maintain a tested recovery approach for releases. A readable pipeline is easier to audit and support during incidents.\n\nImplementation, verification, and operational practice:\nRun the Jenkins pipeline linter, execute a representative test build, and review stage results, reports, and artifacts. Apply a review checklist covering secrets, branch conditions, production approvals, agent labels, timeouts, and failure handling. Record the approved Jenkinsfile version for the release.\n\nIn a production Jenkins environment, validate this behavior with a controlled test pipeline before enabling it for critical workloads. Record the configuration, repository or job involved, expected result, actual result, and relevant console output. Review permissions, credential exposure, network dependencies, agent requirements, and failure behavior. Prefer version-controlled configuration and repeatable procedures over undocumented manual changes. When a failure occurs, preserve the original evidence, identify the first failing operation, correct the underlying cause, and rerun the smallest safe validation before restarting the complete delivery workflow."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Scripted Pipeline",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-3-topic-10-section-1",
        "title": "Scripted Pipeline — Pipeline Architecture and Execution",
        "content": "# Scripted Pipeline — Pipeline Architecture and Execution\n\n## 1. Explanation and Learning Objective\n\nThis section belongs to **Scripted Pipeline** in Stage 03: Jenkins Continuous Integration and Continuous Delivery.\n\nScripted Pipeline uses Groovy-based control flow to define Jenkins automation. It supports programmatic decisions, loops, functions, exception handling, and dynamic pipeline behavior. Its flexibility is useful for complex orchestration, but excessive scripting can make pipelines difficult to maintain and secure. Scripted pipelines should use understandable functions, controlled variable scope, approved shared libraries, and carefully reviewed access to Jenkins APIs.\n\nExplain the internal architecture and responsibility boundaries. Describe how the Jenkins controller, agents, executors, workspaces, queue, job configuration, build records, plugins, and external systems interact where relevant. Identify which component initiates the operation, which component performs the work, and how results return to Jenkins. Explain the impact of network interruption, unavailable agents, insufficient executors, and controller resource pressure.\n\nAfter studying this section, an engineer should be able to explain the concept in plain English, identify the Jenkins components involved, configure or execute the relevant operation, inspect its result, and recognize the risks of incorrect implementation.\n\n## 2. Technical Behavior and Component Responsibilities\n\nJenkins is an automation server. The controller manages configuration, job scheduling, pipeline orchestration, plugins, credentials integration, and build metadata. Agents execute build steps in their workspaces. Executors represent the available execution slots on a node. A pipeline coordinates multiple operations and records their results.\n\nA Jenkins build can interact with source-control systems, build tools, test frameworks, code-quality platforms, container registries, artifact repositories, and deployment environments. Each integration has its own configuration and security boundary.\n\nFor **Scripted Pipeline — Pipeline Architecture and Execution**, identify which component owns the configuration, which component performs the work, what information is passed between components, and where the result is recorded. This separation is important when diagnosing failures: a controller scheduling problem is different from an agent toolchain failure or an application test failure.\n\n## 3. Implementation Workflow\n\n1. Confirm the Jenkins controller URL and the intended job or multibranch pipeline.\n2. Inspect the repository and identify the Jenkinsfile or administrative configuration involved.\n3. Confirm the required Java runtime, build tools, plugins, credentials, and agent labels.\n4. Verify that the executing identity has only the permissions required for the task.\n5. Apply the configuration or pipeline change through the approved review process.\n6. Run a controlled build and observe the queue, assigned agent, workspace, and stage sequence.\n7. Inspect console output and test results; identify the first meaningful failure rather than only the final failed status.\n8. Verify produced artifacts and confirm that they correspond to the intended source revision.\n9. Record the build URL, source commit, artifact version, and validation outcome.\n\nDo not assume that a green pipeline proves that a production deployment is safe. The pipeline must execute the required checks, publish the expected artifact, and respect deployment approvals and environment controls.\n\n## 4. Practical Configuration and Commands\n\nThe following examples are relevant to this section. Replace example hostnames, paths, IDs, and credentials with approved values from the environment.\n\n`\bash\n# Validate the Jenkinsfile using the Jenkins Declarative Linter endpoint.\ncurl -X POST -u USER:TOKEN \\\n  -F \"jenkinsfile=<Jenkinsfile\" \\\n  https://jenkins.example.com/pipeline-model-converter/validate\n`\n\nNever paste real passwords, private keys, API tokens, or production secrets into a Jenkinsfile or terminal command that may be recorded in build logs. Use Jenkins Credentials and approved secret-injection mechanisms.\n\n## 5. Jenkins Pipeline Example\n\nA typical application pipeline can use the following structure:\n\n`groovy\npipeline {\n    agent { label 'linux' }\n\n    options {\n        timestamps()\n        disableConcurrentBuilds()\n    }\n\n    stages {\n        stage('Checkout') {\n            steps {\n                checkout scm\n            }\n        }\n\n        stage('Build') {\n            steps {\n                sh 'mvn -B clean package'\n            }\n        }\n\n        stage('Unit Tests') {\n            steps {\n                sh 'mvn -B test'\n            }\n            post {\n                always {\n                    junit '**/target/surefire-reports/*.xml'\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        stage('Archive Artifact') {\n            steps {\n                archiveArtifacts artifacts: 'target/*.jar',\n                                 fingerprint: true\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    post {\n        success {\n            echo 'Pipeline completed successfully'\n        }\n        failure {\n            echo 'Pipeline failed. Review the failed stage and console log.'\n        }\n        always {\n            echo 'Pipeline execution finished'\n        }\n    }\n}\n`\n\nThis example demonstrates a controlled sequence: source checkout, application build, test execution, report publication, and artifact archiving. A real project must adapt its build command, artifact pattern, agent requirements, and quality gates to the application.\n\n## 6. Production DevOps Scenario\n\nConsider a Java application maintained in GitHub. A developer pushes a feature branch and opens a pull request. The repository webhook notifies Jenkins, which starts the appropriate multibranch pipeline and checks out the proposed revision.\n\nJenkins schedules the build on an agent with the required JDK and Maven versions. The pipeline compiles the source, executes unit tests, publishes test reports, and performs configured quality or security checks. If any required stage fails, the build reports the failure and the pull request cannot satisfy the required CI status.\n\nAfter the change is reviewed and merged, a trusted pipeline builds the approved revision and publishes a versioned artifact. Deployment automation retrieves that exact artifact and applies the organization's approval, environment, and rollback policies.\n\nThe team can trace the deployed version to its source commit, Jenkins build, test evidence, and artifact repository record. This traceability is essential when investigating production incidents or reproducing a release.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes and Their Impact\n\n- Running builds on an overloaded controller can reduce availability and affect other jobs.\n- Assigning an incorrect agent label can leave builds queued or run them on an unsuitable machine.\n- Using incompatible Java, Maven, or plugin versions can break previously working pipelines.\n- Hard-coding credentials in pipeline source can expose secrets to repository users and build logs.\n- Ignoring exit codes can allow a pipeline to report success after a failed command.\n- Using broad artifact patterns can archive unintended files or omit the required output.\n- Sharing mutable workspaces between concurrent builds can cause file collisions.\n- Installing untested plugins can introduce compatibility and security problems.\n- Giving untrusted pull-request builds production credentials can create a serious security exposure.\n- Rebuilding an artifact independently for each environment can produce different binaries from the same source revision.\n\n## 8. Troubleshooting and Safe Recovery\n\nBegin with the Jenkins build URL, job name, source revision, failed stage, console output, and timestamp. Determine whether the failure occurred before agent allocation, during workspace preparation, while executing a command, or while publishing results.\n\nCheck controller logs for scheduling and orchestration issues. Check agent logs and operating-system resources for connectivity, disk, memory, permission, and toolchain problems. Verify plugin compatibility and credential IDs without printing secret values. Confirm that the expected environment variables and tools are available to the executing agent.\n\nCorrect the underlying cause, then rerun a focused build. Preserve logs and failed-build evidence until the incident is understood. Do not delete workspaces, restart services, or rotate credentials blindly when those actions could destroy useful evidence or interrupt unrelated production jobs.\n\n## 9. Security, Reliability, and Operational Best Practices\n\nUse supported Jenkins and Java releases. Maintain a tested plugin inventory and apply security updates through a controlled process. Back up JENKINS_HOME and test restoration. Restrict controller access and avoid executing untrusted build workloads with controller-level privileges.\n\nUse least-privilege credentials, scoped permissions, protected pipeline definitions, and isolated agents. Keep pipeline logic in source control and review it like application code. Apply timeouts, build retention, concurrency controls, and clear failure handling where appropriate.\n\nMonitor controller health, queue length, executor utilization, agent availability, disk usage, build duration, and failure rates. Ensure that artifact retention and deployment traceability meet operational requirements.\n\n## 10. Verification and Completion Criteria\n\nThe work represented by **Scripted Pipeline — Pipeline Architecture and Execution** is complete only when the intended behavior is demonstrated and independently verifiable.\n\nVerify that:\n\n- The correct Jenkins job, pipeline, node, or configuration was used.\n- The expected source revision was checked out.\n- Required stages and steps executed in the intended order.\n- Commands returned the expected exit status.\n- Test reports and relevant quality checks were published.\n- Credentials were referenced securely and not exposed in logs.\n- Required artifacts were produced and can be identified by version.\n- Failure conditions are visible and do not silently become successful builds.\n- The final build record provides enough evidence for another engineer to reproduce the result.\n\n## 11. Practical Exercise\n\nCreate or use a small Java application repository containing a Jenkinsfile. Configure a Jenkins job or multibranch pipeline to check out the repository, compile the application, run tests, and archive the resulting JAR.\n\nRun the pipeline once with valid configuration and record the build URL, source commit, test result, and archived artifact. Then introduce one controlled failure, such as an incorrect test expectation or unavailable build tool. Observe which stage fails and inspect the console output.\n\nCorrect the problem and run the pipeline again. Confirm that the final build succeeds and that the expected artifact and reports are available. Explain what changed between the failed and successful executions.\n\n## 12. Interview and Engineering Understanding\n\nAn engineer should be able to explain what **Scripted Pipeline — Pipeline Architecture and Execution** means, why Jenkins uses it, which components participate, how the implementation works, what configuration is required, and how success or failure is verified.\n\nA strong answer describes the actual execution sequence, the repository or Jenkins state before and after the operation, the evidence collected, and the production risks. Avoid simply naming a Jenkins feature or command. Explain the engineering reason for using it and how it contributes to reliable software delivery."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-3-topic-10-section-2",
        "title": "Scripted Pipeline — Controller and Agent Responsibilities",
        "content": "# Scripted Pipeline — Controller and Agent Responsibilities\n\n## 1. Explanation and Learning Objective\n\nThis section belongs to **Scripted Pipeline** in Stage 03: Jenkins Continuous Integration and Continuous Delivery.\n\nScripted Pipeline uses Groovy-based control flow to define Jenkins automation. It supports programmatic decisions, loops, functions, exception handling, and dynamic pipeline behavior. Its flexibility is useful for complex orchestration, but excessive scripting can make pipelines difficult to maintain and secure. Scripted pipelines should use understandable functions, controlled variable scope, approved shared libraries, and carefully reviewed access to Jenkins APIs.\n\nExplain the internal architecture and responsibility boundaries. Describe how the Jenkins controller, agents, executors, workspaces, queue, job configuration, build records, plugins, and external systems interact where relevant. Identify which component initiates the operation, which component performs the work, and how results return to Jenkins. Explain the impact of network interruption, unavailable agents, insufficient executors, and controller resource pressure.\n\nAfter studying this section, an engineer should be able to explain the concept in plain English, identify the Jenkins components involved, configure or execute the relevant operation, inspect its result, and recognize the risks of incorrect implementation.\n\n## 2. Technical Behavior and Component Responsibilities\n\nJenkins is an automation server. The controller manages configuration, job scheduling, pipeline orchestration, plugins, credentials integration, and build metadata. Agents execute build steps in their workspaces. Executors represent the available execution slots on a node. A pipeline coordinates multiple operations and records their results.\n\nA Jenkins build can interact with source-control systems, build tools, test frameworks, code-quality platforms, container registries, artifact repositories, and deployment environments. Each integration has its own configuration and security boundary.\n\nFor **Scripted Pipeline — Controller and Agent Responsibilities**, identify which component owns the configuration, which component performs the work, what information is passed between components, and where the result is recorded. This separation is important when diagnosing failures: a controller scheduling problem is different from an agent toolchain failure or an application test failure.\n\n## 3. Implementation Workflow\n\n1. Confirm the Jenkins controller URL and the intended job or multibranch pipeline.\n2. Inspect the repository and identify the Jenkinsfile or administrative configuration involved.\n3. Confirm the required Java runtime, build tools, plugins, credentials, and agent labels.\n4. Verify that the executing identity has only the permissions required for the task.\n5. Apply the configuration or pipeline change through the approved review process.\n6. Run a controlled build and observe the queue, assigned agent, workspace, and stage sequence.\n7. Inspect console output and test results; identify the first meaningful failure rather than only the final failed status.\n8. Verify produced artifacts and confirm that they correspond to the intended source revision.\n9. Record the build URL, source commit, artifact version, and validation outcome.\n\nDo not assume that a green pipeline proves that a production deployment is safe. The pipeline must execute the required checks, publish the expected artifact, and respect deployment approvals and environment controls.\n\n## 4. Practical Configuration and Commands\n\nThe following examples are relevant to this section. Replace example hostnames, paths, IDs, and credentials with approved values from the environment.\n\n`\bash\njava -version\nhostname\nwhoami\ndf -h\nfree -h\njava -jar agent.jar -url https://jenkins.example.com/ -secret <agent-secret> -name linux-agent -workDir \"/opt/jenkins\"\n`\n\nNever paste real passwords, private keys, API tokens, or production secrets into a Jenkinsfile or terminal command that may be recorded in build logs. Use Jenkins Credentials and approved secret-injection mechanisms.\n\n## 5. Jenkins Pipeline Example\n\nA typical application pipeline can use the following structure:\n\n`groovy\npipeline {\n    agent { label 'linux' }\n\n    options {\n        timestamps()\n        disableConcurrentBuilds()\n    }\n\n    stages {\n        stage('Checkout') {\n            steps {\n                checkout scm\n            }\n        }\n\n        stage('Build') {\n            steps {\n                sh 'mvn -B clean package'\n            }\n        }\n\n        stage('Unit Tests') {\n            steps {\n                sh 'mvn -B test'\n            }\n            post {\n                always {\n                    junit '**/target/surefire-reports/*.xml'\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        stage('Archive Artifact') {\n            steps {\n                archiveArtifacts artifacts: 'target/*.jar',\n                                 fingerprint: true\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    post {\n        success {\n            echo 'Pipeline completed successfully'\n        }\n        failure {\n            echo 'Pipeline failed. Review the failed stage and console log.'\n        }\n        always {\n            echo 'Pipeline execution finished'\n        }\n    }\n}\n`\n\nThis example demonstrates a controlled sequence: source checkout, application build, test execution, report publication, and artifact archiving. A real project must adapt its build command, artifact pattern, agent requirements, and quality gates to the application.\n\n## 6. Production DevOps Scenario\n\nConsider a Java application maintained in GitHub. A developer pushes a feature branch and opens a pull request. The repository webhook notifies Jenkins, which starts the appropriate multibranch pipeline and checks out the proposed revision.\n\nJenkins schedules the build on an agent with the required JDK and Maven versions. The pipeline compiles the source, executes unit tests, publishes test reports, and performs configured quality or security checks. If any required stage fails, the build reports the failure and the pull request cannot satisfy the required CI status.\n\nAfter the change is reviewed and merged, a trusted pipeline builds the approved revision and publishes a versioned artifact. Deployment automation retrieves that exact artifact and applies the organization's approval, environment, and rollback policies.\n\nThe team can trace the deployed version to its source commit, Jenkins build, test evidence, and artifact repository record. This traceability is essential when investigating production incidents or reproducing a release.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes and Their Impact\n\n- Running builds on an overloaded controller can reduce availability and affect other jobs.\n- Assigning an incorrect agent label can leave builds queued or run them on an unsuitable machine.\n- Using incompatible Java, Maven, or plugin versions can break previously working pipelines.\n- Hard-coding credentials in pipeline source can expose secrets to repository users and build logs.\n- Ignoring exit codes can allow a pipeline to report success after a failed command.\n- Using broad artifact patterns can archive unintended files or omit the required output.\n- Sharing mutable workspaces between concurrent builds can cause file collisions.\n- Installing untested plugins can introduce compatibility and security problems.\n- Giving untrusted pull-request builds production credentials can create a serious security exposure.\n- Rebuilding an artifact independently for each environment can produce different binaries from the same source revision.\n\n## 8. Troubleshooting and Safe Recovery\n\nBegin with the Jenkins build URL, job name, source revision, failed stage, console output, and timestamp. Determine whether the failure occurred before agent allocation, during workspace preparation, while executing a command, or while publishing results.\n\nCheck controller logs for scheduling and orchestration issues. Check agent logs and operating-system resources for connectivity, disk, memory, permission, and toolchain problems. Verify plugin compatibility and credential IDs without printing secret values. Confirm that the expected environment variables and tools are available to the executing agent.\n\nCorrect the underlying cause, then rerun a focused build. Preserve logs and failed-build evidence until the incident is understood. Do not delete workspaces, restart services, or rotate credentials blindly when those actions could destroy useful evidence or interrupt unrelated production jobs.\n\n## 9. Security, Reliability, and Operational Best Practices\n\nUse supported Jenkins and Java releases. Maintain a tested plugin inventory and apply security updates through a controlled process. Back up JENKINS_HOME and test restoration. Restrict controller access and avoid executing untrusted build workloads with controller-level privileges.\n\nUse least-privilege credentials, scoped permissions, protected pipeline definitions, and isolated agents. Keep pipeline logic in source control and review it like application code. Apply timeouts, build retention, concurrency controls, and clear failure handling where appropriate.\n\nMonitor controller health, queue length, executor utilization, agent availability, disk usage, build duration, and failure rates. Ensure that artifact retention and deployment traceability meet operational requirements.\n\n## 10. Verification and Completion Criteria\n\nThe work represented by **Scripted Pipeline — Controller and Agent Responsibilities** is complete only when the intended behavior is demonstrated and independently verifiable.\n\nVerify that:\n\n- The correct Jenkins job, pipeline, node, or configuration was used.\n- The expected source revision was checked out.\n- Required stages and steps executed in the intended order.\n- Commands returned the expected exit status.\n- Test reports and relevant quality checks were published.\n- Credentials were referenced securely and not exposed in logs.\n- Required artifacts were produced and can be identified by version.\n- Failure conditions are visible and do not silently become successful builds.\n- The final build record provides enough evidence for another engineer to reproduce the result.\n\n## 11. Practical Exercise\n\nCreate or use a small Java application repository containing a Jenkinsfile. Configure a Jenkins job or multibranch pipeline to check out the repository, compile the application, run tests, and archive the resulting JAR.\n\nRun the pipeline once with valid configuration and record the build URL, source commit, test result, and archived artifact. Then introduce one controlled failure, such as an incorrect test expectation or unavailable build tool. Observe which stage fails and inspect the console output.\n\nCorrect the problem and run the pipeline again. Confirm that the final build succeeds and that the expected artifact and reports are available. Explain what changed between the failed and successful executions.\n\n## 12. Interview and Engineering Understanding\n\nAn engineer should be able to explain what **Scripted Pipeline — Controller and Agent Responsibilities** means, why Jenkins uses it, which components participate, how the implementation works, what configuration is required, and how success or failure is verified.\n\nA strong answer describes the actual execution sequence, the repository or Jenkins state before and after the operation, the evidence collected, and the production risks. Avoid simply naming a Jenkins feature or command. Explain the engineering reason for using it and how it contributes to reliable software delivery."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-3-topic-10-section-3",
        "title": "Scripted Pipeline — Installation and Configuration",
        "content": "# Scripted Pipeline — Installation and Configuration\n\n## 1. Explanation and Learning Objective\n\nThis section belongs to **Scripted Pipeline** in Stage 03: Jenkins Continuous Integration and Continuous Delivery.\n\nScripted Pipeline uses Groovy-based control flow to define Jenkins automation. It supports programmatic decisions, loops, functions, exception handling, and dynamic pipeline behavior. Its flexibility is useful for complex orchestration, but excessive scripting can make pipelines difficult to maintain and secure. Scripted pipelines should use understandable functions, controlled variable scope, approved shared libraries, and carefully reviewed access to Jenkins APIs.\n\nDescribe the implementation prerequisites and configuration sequence. Cover supported Java runtime requirements, Jenkins installation or container deployment, service startup, persistent JENKINS_HOME storage, initial administrator configuration, plugin selection, network access, and system permissions as applicable. Explain how to verify the installed version, service health, configured URL, storage availability, and required build tools. Include upgrade and backup considerations.\n\nAfter studying this section, an engineer should be able to explain the concept in plain English, identify the Jenkins components involved, configure or execute the relevant operation, inspect its result, and recognize the risks of incorrect implementation.\n\n## 2. Technical Behavior and Component Responsibilities\n\nJenkins is an automation server. The controller manages configuration, job scheduling, pipeline orchestration, plugins, credentials integration, and build metadata. Agents execute build steps in their workspaces. Executors represent the available execution slots on a node. A pipeline coordinates multiple operations and records their results.\n\nA Jenkins build can interact with source-control systems, build tools, test frameworks, code-quality platforms, container registries, artifact repositories, and deployment environments. Each integration has its own configuration and security boundary.\n\nFor **Scripted Pipeline — Installation and Configuration**, identify which component owns the configuration, which component performs the work, what information is passed between components, and where the result is recorded. This separation is important when diagnosing failures: a controller scheduling problem is different from an agent toolchain failure or an application test failure.\n\n## 3. Implementation Workflow\n\n1. Confirm the Jenkins controller URL and the intended job or multibranch pipeline.\n2. Inspect the repository and identify the Jenkinsfile or administrative configuration involved.\n3. Confirm the required Java runtime, build tools, plugins, credentials, and agent labels.\n4. Verify that the executing identity has only the permissions required for the task.\n5. Apply the configuration or pipeline change through the approved review process.\n6. Run a controlled build and observe the queue, assigned agent, workspace, and stage sequence.\n7. Inspect console output and test results; identify the first meaningful failure rather than only the final failed status.\n8. Verify produced artifacts and confirm that they correspond to the intended source revision.\n9. Record the build URL, source commit, artifact version, and validation outcome.\n\nDo not assume that a green pipeline proves that a production deployment is safe. The pipeline must execute the required checks, publish the expected artifact, and respect deployment approvals and environment controls.\n\n## 4. Practical Configuration and Commands\n\nThe following examples are relevant to this section. Replace example hostnames, paths, IDs, and credentials with approved values from the environment.\n\n`\bash\n# Validate the Jenkinsfile using the Jenkins Declarative Linter endpoint.\ncurl -X POST -u USER:TOKEN \\\n  -F \"jenkinsfile=<Jenkinsfile\" \\\n  https://jenkins.example.com/pipeline-model-converter/validate\n`\n\nNever paste real passwords, private keys, API tokens, or production secrets into a Jenkinsfile or terminal command that may be recorded in build logs. Use Jenkins Credentials and approved secret-injection mechanisms.\n\n## 5. Jenkins Pipeline Example\n\nA typical application pipeline can use the following structure:\n\n`groovy\npipeline {\n    agent { label 'linux' }\n\n    options {\n        timestamps()\n        disableConcurrentBuilds()\n    }\n\n    stages {\n        stage('Checkout') {\n            steps {\n                checkout scm\n            }\n        }\n\n        stage('Build') {\n            steps {\n                sh 'mvn -B clean package'\n            }\n        }\n\n        stage('Unit Tests') {\n            steps {\n                sh 'mvn -B test'\n            }\n            post {\n                always {\n                    junit '**/target/surefire-reports/*.xml'\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        stage('Archive Artifact') {\n            steps {\n                archiveArtifacts artifacts: 'target/*.jar',\n                                 fingerprint: true\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    post {\n        success {\n            echo 'Pipeline completed successfully'\n        }\n        failure {\n            echo 'Pipeline failed. Review the failed stage and console log.'\n        }\n        always {\n            echo 'Pipeline execution finished'\n        }\n    }\n}\n`\n\nThis example demonstrates a controlled sequence: source checkout, application build, test execution, report publication, and artifact archiving. A real project must adapt its build command, artifact pattern, agent requirements, and quality gates to the application.\n\n## 6. Production DevOps Scenario\n\nConsider a Java application maintained in GitHub. A developer pushes a feature branch and opens a pull request. The repository webhook notifies Jenkins, which starts the appropriate multibranch pipeline and checks out the proposed revision.\n\nJenkins schedules the build on an agent with the required JDK and Maven versions. The pipeline compiles the source, executes unit tests, publishes test reports, and performs configured quality or security checks. If any required stage fails, the build reports the failure and the pull request cannot satisfy the required CI status.\n\nAfter the change is reviewed and merged, a trusted pipeline builds the approved revision and publishes a versioned artifact. Deployment automation retrieves that exact artifact and applies the organization's approval, environment, and rollback policies.\n\nThe team can trace the deployed version to its source commit, Jenkins build, test evidence, and artifact repository record. This traceability is essential when investigating production incidents or reproducing a release.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes and Their Impact\n\n- Running builds on an overloaded controller can reduce availability and affect other jobs.\n- Assigning an incorrect agent label can leave builds queued or run them on an unsuitable machine.\n- Using incompatible Java, Maven, or plugin versions can break previously working pipelines.\n- Hard-coding credentials in pipeline source can expose secrets to repository users and build logs.\n- Ignoring exit codes can allow a pipeline to report success after a failed command.\n- Using broad artifact patterns can archive unintended files or omit the required output.\n- Sharing mutable workspaces between concurrent builds can cause file collisions.\n- Installing untested plugins can introduce compatibility and security problems.\n- Giving untrusted pull-request builds production credentials can create a serious security exposure.\n- Rebuilding an artifact independently for each environment can produce different binaries from the same source revision.\n\n## 8. Troubleshooting and Safe Recovery\n\nBegin with the Jenkins build URL, job name, source revision, failed stage, console output, and timestamp. Determine whether the failure occurred before agent allocation, during workspace preparation, while executing a command, or while publishing results.\n\nCheck controller logs for scheduling and orchestration issues. Check agent logs and operating-system resources for connectivity, disk, memory, permission, and toolchain problems. Verify plugin compatibility and credential IDs without printing secret values. Confirm that the expected environment variables and tools are available to the executing agent.\n\nCorrect the underlying cause, then rerun a focused build. Preserve logs and failed-build evidence until the incident is understood. Do not delete workspaces, restart services, or rotate credentials blindly when those actions could destroy useful evidence or interrupt unrelated production jobs.\n\n## 9. Security, Reliability, and Operational Best Practices\n\nUse supported Jenkins and Java releases. Maintain a tested plugin inventory and apply security updates through a controlled process. Back up JENKINS_HOME and test restoration. Restrict controller access and avoid executing untrusted build workloads with controller-level privileges.\n\nUse least-privilege credentials, scoped permissions, protected pipeline definitions, and isolated agents. Keep pipeline logic in source control and review it like application code. Apply timeouts, build retention, concurrency controls, and clear failure handling where appropriate.\n\nMonitor controller health, queue length, executor utilization, agent availability, disk usage, build duration, and failure rates. Ensure that artifact retention and deployment traceability meet operational requirements.\n\n## 10. Verification and Completion Criteria\n\nThe work represented by **Scripted Pipeline — Installation and Configuration** is complete only when the intended behavior is demonstrated and independently verifiable.\n\nVerify that:\n\n- The correct Jenkins job, pipeline, node, or configuration was used.\n- The expected source revision was checked out.\n- Required stages and steps executed in the intended order.\n- Commands returned the expected exit status.\n- Test reports and relevant quality checks were published.\n- Credentials were referenced securely and not exposed in logs.\n- Required artifacts were produced and can be identified by version.\n- Failure conditions are visible and do not silently become successful builds.\n- The final build record provides enough evidence for another engineer to reproduce the result.\n\n## 11. Practical Exercise\n\nCreate or use a small Java application repository containing a Jenkinsfile. Configure a Jenkins job or multibranch pipeline to check out the repository, compile the application, run tests, and archive the resulting JAR.\n\nRun the pipeline once with valid configuration and record the build URL, source commit, test result, and archived artifact. Then introduce one controlled failure, such as an incorrect test expectation or unavailable build tool. Observe which stage fails and inspect the console output.\n\nCorrect the problem and run the pipeline again. Confirm that the final build succeeds and that the expected artifact and reports are available. Explain what changed between the failed and successful executions.\n\n## 12. Interview and Engineering Understanding\n\nAn engineer should be able to explain what **Scripted Pipeline — Installation and Configuration** means, why Jenkins uses it, which components participate, how the implementation works, what configuration is required, and how success or failure is verified.\n\nA strong answer describes the actual execution sequence, the repository or Jenkins state before and after the operation, the evidence collected, and the production risks. Avoid simply naming a Jenkins feature or command. Explain the engineering reason for using it and how it contributes to reliable software delivery."
